---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/email-design";
const title = "Email design for Shopify lifecycle messages";
const desc = "Make the product, offer and next action understandable in the message a customer actually receives. Design owns rendering, content fallbacks and reusable modules; it cannot repair an incorrect audience or an expired offer.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is one layout suitable for every flow?",
    "answer": "A shared brand shell is useful, but the content hierarchy should fit the task. Product recovery and care instructions need different modules."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a product block guarantee current stock?",
    "answer": "No. Verify when its data are resolved and what happens when the referenced product or variant becomes unavailable."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a rendering check prove revenue improvement?",
    "answer": "No. It demonstrates the inspected rendering behavior. Commercial impact needs a suitable comparison and consistent audience and offer definitions."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Workflow playbook</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Start with one message purpose</h2>
    <p>Write the action before opening the editor: resume a specific cart, understand product care, compare a collection or choose a gift. Give each purpose an appropriate module family rather than forcing an education message into a promotional grid. Product count follows the decision being made and the readable space available, not a universal limit on cards.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Specify product and variant fallbacks</h2>
    <p>Define the source of the name, image, price, currency and destination for each card. Use the selected variant when known; otherwise describe the product without implying a selected size or finish. Missing images need a useful fallback. Decide whether unavailable items disappear, become a truthful restock invitation or stop the send; test an empty recommendation set as carefully as a full grid.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Distinguish catalog snapshots from live content</h2>
    <p>Ask when product information is resolved: template save, message generation, delivery or open. Do not call a saved catalog block live inventory. Recheck commercial terms at the point your system supports, and make the landing page the place to confirm current availability. A price change between dispatch and click needs an understandable customer path, not an implied reservation.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Keep the essential message readable without images</h2>
    <p>Use text for the offer, critical conditions and primary link. Write alternative text that conveys the useful product information without repeating surrounding copy; decorative imagery should not add noise. Review reading order and descriptive links with assistive reading tools. Keep any plain-text part consistent with the product, terms and destination rather than assuming generated text is complete.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Inspect narrow screens and real inboxes</h2>
    <p>Choose a rendering matrix from your audience and reported defects: narrow and wide screens, relevant mail clients, images blocked and light or dark appearance. Inspect wrapping, horizontal overflow, tap spacing and text enlargement on actual received messages. Editor previews are useful for early iteration but do not establish received-client behavior. Save the client and version with each defect.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Treat branding and motion as conditional assets</h2>
    <p>Use font fallbacks that leave headings and buttons readable when the preferred font is unavailable. Check transparent logos against changed backgrounds, including dark appearance. Motion needs a meaningful first frame and a static alternative where needed. Optimize image size against readable product detail and loading conditions; an arbitrary asset limit is not a deliverability guarantee.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Make reusable modules safe to change</h2>
    <p>Keep a versioned shell for header, footer, buttons and offer conditions, with a named owner and a record of which active templates use it. Verify whether editing a shared module updates live flows immediately or only later copies. Local exceptions for regulated copy or special product instructions need an owner so a global refresh does not overwrite them.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Check the whole click destination</h2>
    <p>Send fixtures with long product names, sale pricing, missing images, multiple variants and localized currency. Follow tracked links to the intended product and selection, inspect the checkout terms and verify that preferences and unsubscribe links remain usable. Match subject and preheader to the body. An attractive button cannot compensate for a destination that resets the selected variant.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Separate design evidence from conversion claims</h2>
    <p>Record rendering defects and task completion before interpreting click performance. Compare equivalent template purposes and cohorts; changes in audience, offer or device mix can change click rates without a layout effect. A randomized layout comparison can help estimate commercial impact, but a screenshot or an editor approval is evidence of neither incremental orders nor inbox placement.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Release a template family with a rollback copy</h2>
    <p>Proposed exercise: publish a revised cart shell to controlled recipients, change a catalog image, then remove the referenced variant and resend the fixture. Record the received output and fallback for each case. Retain the prior template and restore it if critical content disappears. Compare supplier tools on this edit-and-recovery task, including export, review effort and paid rendering services, before expanding to other flows.</p>
  </div></section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related workflows"><a class="link" href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">Offer terms</a><a class="link" href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">Product interest</a><a class="link" href="/use-cases/holiday-gifting">Gift creative</a></nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
